+**B.1 The prize, as the problem states it.** For any compact simple gauge group G, a non-trivial quantum Yang–Mills theory exists on ℝ⁴ — existence meaning the Wightman/Osterwalder–Schrader axioms with the theory's local gauge-invariant observables — and has a mass gap Δ > 0: the Hamiltonian's spectrum has 0 as a simple eigenvalue (the vacuum) and nothing in (0, Δ). The physics it names: the strong force has no massless excitation; the lightest glueball is massive; colour is confined.
+
+**B.2 The operator's framing of the same prize.** The proof is to be an infinite function built from finite ones, every step proved infinitely as well, every III breakthrough used on the way proven and credited, and every theorem proved here invoked finitely by the concurrent seat — and vice versa.
+
+**B.3 Where the tree stands today, in the exact words (the parent page's 13:21 note).** On every finite cross-section of square plaquettes, for every finite group with real orthogonal representations, under the linear action, every polynomial action with nonnegative coefficients and the Wilson action e^{βχ} (β ≥ 0), the nonabelian Wilson loop of the x-transfer model converges at every R to a moment sequence on the transfer matrix's spectrum, and for loops with W(1) > 0 its static potential E(T) = lim −log W(R)/R is strictly positive and at least the finite-volume mass gap of its sector; for Z₂ at 0 < y < 1 with no hypothesis left. 313 theorems in 23 files; 35 in the bare kernel (FluxCore 15, Z2Inversion 6, Z2Moment 14), 278 above Mathlib v4.35.0; gate 27 arms / 10 teeth; seventeen landings on 10-27, the last 0ce8207fe and a0d7c07ad.
+
