+**THE LEMMA (the scale-uniform inequality, one statement).** Fix a_c on the construction's sequence so that the blocked Wilson coupling there is β_c ≤ β*. Write the blocked action at a_c as Wilson(β_c) + Σ_C δ_C, the sum over the connected plaquette sets C of its non-Wilson couplings (a single-plaquette correction is a class function and is re-priced exactly by its own character coefficients, so it is not content; the content is the multi-plaquette part). Define the per-plaquette NON-WILSON CONTENT c(β_c) := sup_p Σ_{C ∋ p} (e^{‖δ_C‖_∞} − 1)·e^{α|C|}, and THE EXIT BUDGET ε(β_c) := the largest per-plaquette tilt under which the flux-surface gas's Kotecký–Preiss inequality at β_c still holds with the rate α, every polymer's activity multiplied by e^{ε|X|}. **If c(β_c) ≤ ε(β_c) uniformly along the sequence, then the blocked measure at a_c has a convergent polymer expansion with the rate α for every cutoff — Hypothesis I in the form Theorem 7.4 consumes — and with Hypothesis II (exact under the perfect decimation) the chain of MassGapChain.lean closes with one physical rate.** Proof shape: e^{δ_C} = 1 + (e^{δ_C} − 1) is a bond joining the plaquettes of C; the tilted gas's polymers are the old polymers joined by such bonds; Kotecký–Preiss's tree bound with the bond activity (e^{‖δ_C‖} − 1)e^{α|C|} per plaquette is the stability of a convergent cluster expansion under a perturbation of its activities (classical in shape: Kotecký–Preiss 1986, the two-weight form of S1 §16). The lemma's content is the two numbers.
+
+**The constant, and why it is the object.** ε(β_c) is COMPUTABLE NOW — the certificate's own inequality with a tilt parameter, decided in directed rounding at every β in the disk; a table, largest deep in the disk, zero at β*. c(β_c) is THE EXIT CONTENT, the non-Wilson part of the blocked action at the exit scale uniformly in the cutoff: the window theorem's exit condition, now a number to compare with ε. The β ceiling moves neither: raising β* moves the coupling at which ε vanishes by one step; the crossing is c ≤ ε at SOME disk coupling, and the deeper the exit, the larger the budget and the more steps the content has to decay.
