+**§12 (b) RESULTS — THE PLANAR TWO-STEP OBJECT READ (2026-11-01 12:1x EST, read from `date`; the trunk seat; planar_n4.sh, the control MET — the planar one-step unsliced against sliced, 12 line pairs, max difference 0; records w2n4_W2L2_planar.2026-11-01a.{ordered,cancel,random1}.txt).** (P1) HELD: at the ordered datum the planar two-step equals the bent two-step in all 32 compared values (the nine class moments, the channels, the connected moments, Z) to the last digit (max absolute difference 1.9 × 10⁻³³). (P2) REFUTED AS STAKED, and the refutation is a lemma: at the CANCELLING datum too the two objects agree to every digit — (½,½) mode 1.281822626 × 10⁻², (1,1) 9.279277296 × 10⁻⁵, sup|δ₂| 0.07008, r_mod 3.141591 × 10⁻⁴, E[χ_½(A)] 0.1107069963534, all identical — because the engine's cancelling datum is DEFINED on the shared link's four staples (the four plaquettes at the shared edge outside the object, assigned the four values in the staple enumeration's order), and the planar and the bent objects are the same abstract complex (a 2n × n rectangle, folded or flat) with the same staple census and the same staple VALUES in the same slots at every datum supported on the shared link's staples: their Haar integrals coincide by construction, at the ordered datum and at the cancelling one alike, and §7 (2)'s "they part only at a non-trivial datum" is sharpened to "only at a datum supported off the shared link's staples". (P3) FAILED NARROWLY: at the Haar-random datum (seed 1) the objects part — the planar (½,½) mode −2.267 × 10⁻⁴ against the bent +5.661 × 10⁻⁵ (a factor 4.0, the stake said 3; the signs opposite), sup|δ₂| 9.1 × 10⁻⁴ against 2.3 × 10⁻⁴, E[χ_½(A)] −1.58 × 10⁻³ against −0.73 × 10⁻³ — two different outside sets, as the stake said, their magnitudes of one order. (P4) HELD: the (2,2) peak under full reads 1,037,232 at every datum, the bent's number. What it says: the coplanar pair's coupling is the bent pair's at every datum the engine's two named data provide